course: dictionary
lesson: 4290
title: The Gregorian calendar
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A calendar is a system for counting days, weeks, months and years. The Gregorian calendar is the one most of the world uses today. «This Gregorian calendar is the one most of the world uses today.» So when you write the date on your homework, you are most likely using it.
explain: Pope Gregory XIII brought in the Gregorian calendar in 1582. The clue is in the name: Gregorian comes from Gregory. «In 1582 Pope Gregory XIII brought in the Gregorian calendar.»
explain: The same word also names a style of singing. Gregorian chant is an old, plain style of church singing without instruments, named after Pope Gregory I. Gregory I and Gregory XIII are two different popes, so always check which one a text means.
explain: The calendar has a leap year rule. A year is a leap year every 4 years, except century years, which must divide exactly by 400. «2000 was a leap year but 1900 was not.» Try the rule yourself and see if it fits both years.
fact: Gregorian means linked to Pope Gregory.
fact: The Gregorian calendar is the one most of the world uses today.
fact: Pope Gregory XIII introduced the Gregorian calendar in 1582, with leap years.
fact: Gregorian chant is old, plain church singing, sung without instruments.
fact: «Russia and Ukraine switched in 1918» to the Gregorian calendar, so old papers may show both «O.S.» (old style) and «N.S.» (new style) dates.
exercise: Which calendar is the one most of the world uses today? | *The Gregorian calendar | The school calendar | The moon calendar | The Julian calendar
exercise: «1900 was not a leap year.» Why not? | Because it was before 1582 | *Because it is a century year that does not divide exactly by 400 | Because Pope Gregory said so | Because it is not divisible by 4 at all
exercise: Which name is for plain church singing with no instruments, named after Pope Gregory I? | Julian calendar | Gregorian calendar | Leap year | *Gregorian chant
test: «In 1582 Thursday 4 October was followed by Friday 15 October.» How many dates were skipped? | 1 | *10 | 4 | 13
test: «Shakespeare and Cervantes both died on 23 April 1616, but England used the Julian calendar and Spain the Gregorian.» Why were they about 10 days apart? | Their clocks were set wrongly | *England and Spain used different calendars, so the same date was not the same day | They died in different years | Their calendars were different, so the same date fell on different days
exam: «Which year was a leap year in the Gregorian calendar?» | 1700 | 1900 | *2000 | 1800
